          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "122"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.069,
            0.489,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.107,
            0.886,
            0.18
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In about 150 CE, Ptolemy of Alexandria refined Archimedes computations for use in his astronomical tables, giving the ratio "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 3 7 7 } { 1 2 0 }   \\approx   3 . 1 4 1 6 7"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\pi ."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.18,
            0.886,
            0.401
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Shortly thereafter, in China, a ‘circle-cutting method’ of "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Liu Hui",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (263 CE) laid the groundwork for two breakthroughs due to "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Zu Chongzhi",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (480 CE). Zu pushed the polygon approximation method to 24,576 sides! He used this to discover the Yuelü (Approximate Ratio) of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "{ \\frac { 2 2 } { 7 } } \\approx 3 . 1 4 2 8"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\pi ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and also the Miü (Close Ratio) of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 3 5 5 } { 1 1 3 } \\approx 3 . 1 4 1 5 9 2 9"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for π. The rational fraction "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac{355}{113}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is so close to π that it remained the most accurate value for π in the world for over 800 years; it is now known that no single rational fraction with denominator less than 15,000 can be as close to π as this fraction!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.402,
            0.887,
            0.598
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In 499 CE, Āryabhaṭa provided a value of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac{62832}{20000} \\in 3.1416"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for π. Crucially, he described this value as asanna, i.e., ‘approaching’ or ‘approximate’ —  a profound insight suggesting that the ratio could not be given exactly as one simple fraction. Meanwhile, "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Brahmagupta",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (628 CE) suggested the use of the value "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.1622"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\pi ;"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " while slightly less accurate, he chose it for its mathematical elegance and the ease with which it could be manipulated in equations, a preference that saw "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\sqrt { 1 0 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " become the dominant approximation in the Arab world and medieval Europe for centuries after."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.598,
            0.886,
            0.757
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Over the ensuing years, many great mathematicians studied "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\pi ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " including its approximations; but no approximation was as accurate as "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{Zn}^{\\prime} \\mathrm{s} \\xrightarrow[113]{355} \\mathrm{一 or}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " as algebraically simple as "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 2 2 } { 7 }   , 3 . 1 4 1 6 ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "$\\sqrt { 1 0 }$  — until"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " the work of Mādhava of Sangamagrāma, who changed the game forever. Mādhava realised that π was not just a number to be approximated by fractions and other finite algebraic expressions, but a limit to be reached. Mādhava thereby discovered the first exact formula for π:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.383,
            0.762,
            0.618,
            0.797
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_4_equation_6.jpg",
          "content": "\\frac{\\pi}{4}=1-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{7}+\\cdots"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.802,
            0.887,
            0.881
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Is that not a beautiful formula? Mādhava’s formula — given in the form of an ‘infinite series’ — was a tectonic shift for mathematics. By moving from the geometric cutting of circles to the analytical summing of numbers, Mādhava birthed the area of mathematics"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
